    <!-- PROJECT 1: METROLOGY & CAD -->
    <section id="metrology" class="section">
      <div class="project-card">
        <h2>📐 Precision Metrology & Reverse Engineering</h2>
        <h3>Dimensional Inspection, Tolerance Analysis & CAD Modeling</h3>
        
        <div class="card-layout-grid">
          <div class="text-column-left">
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Objective</h4>
              <p>
                Obtain high-accuracy dimensional measurements across 20 distinct features (Locations A–T) of a mechanical knob to reconstruct a fully constrained, manufacturable CAD model. Evaluated how instrument resolution, feature geometry, and allowable tolerances govern component fit and function.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Methodology & Technical Execution</h4>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Multi-Instrument Calibration:</strong> Measured external, internal, and depth features using Dial Calipers, Micrometers, Gauge Pins, Gauge Blocks, and an Optical Comparator.</li>
                <li><strong>Uncertainty & Capability Analysis:</strong> Compared tool precision limits against feature scale—balancing high-resolution localized tools with broader-range instruments.</li>
                <li><strong>Tolerance Stack-Up Evaluation:</strong> Defined engineering tolerances to ensure proper clearance and interference fits across mating assemblies.</li>
                <li><strong>CAD Reconstruction:</strong> Translated empirical inspection data into a precise 3D digital solid model ready for manufacturing validation.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="media-column-right">
            <div class="img-frame">
              <!-- Replace with your CAD knob drawing or optical comparator photo -->
              <img src="metrology-knob.png" alt="Precision Measurement and CAD Reconstruction of Mechanical Knob">
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Tools & Competencies</h4>
              <div class="skills">
                <span class="tag">Micrometer & Calipers</span>
                <span class="tag">Optical Comparator</span>
                <span class="tag">Gauge Pins & Blocks</span>
                <span class="tag">GD&T / Tolerancing</span>
                <span class="tag">CAD Reverse Engineering</span>
                <span class="tag">Measurement Uncertainty</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROJECT 2: METAL FORMING & TENSILE TESTING -->
    <section id="metal-forming" class="section">
      <div class="project-card">
        <h2>🔨 Sheet Metal Forming & Mechanical Characterization</h2>
        <h3>Plastic Deformation, Spot Welding & Tensile Failure Analysis</h3>
        
        <div class="card-layout-grid">
          <div class="text-column-left">
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Objective</h4>
              <p>
                Investigate how material yield strength, ductility, and strain hardening govern formability without fracture. Combined hands-on sheet metal fabrication using Carbon Steel 1008 with destructive uniaxial tensile testing of Aluminum 6061.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Methodology & Technical Execution</h4>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Sheet Metal Layout & Bending:</strong> Marked bend allowances on Carbon Steel 1008 using a combination square and scriber, then sheared and formed geometry on a sheet metal bender.</li>
                <li><strong>Resistance Spot Welding:</strong> Joined structural seams of the fabricated enclosure using calibrated resistance spot welding parameters.</li>
                <li><strong>Uniaxial Tensile Testing:</strong> Pulled an Aluminum 6061 dog-bone specimen to fracture to capture continuous load-displacement data.</li>
                <li><strong>Stress–Strain Curve Analysis:</strong> Calculated Modulus of Elasticity, 0.2% offset Yield Strength, Ultimate Tensile Strength (UTS), and percent elongation to map elastic-to-plastic transitions.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="media-column-right">
            <div class="img-frame">
              <!-- Replace with your sheet metal box or stress-strain plot -->
              <img src="metal-forming.png" alt="Carbon Steel Sheet Metal Box and Aluminum 6061 Stress-Strain Curve">
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Tools & Competencies</h4>
              <div class="skills">
                <span class="tag">Carbon Steel 1008</span>
                <span class="tag">Aluminum 6061</span>
                <span class="tag">Sheet Metal Bending</span>
                <span class="tag">Spot Welding</span>
                <span class="tag">Tensile Testing</span>
                <span class="tag">Stress–Strain Analysis</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROJECT 3: INJECTION MOLDING & MILLING -->
    <section id="injection-molding" class="section">
      <div class="project-card">
        <h2>⚙️ Injection Mold Insert Fabrication & Machining Physics</h2>
        <h3>Manual Milling, Cutting Force Modeling & Surface Roughness</h3>
        
        <div class="card-layout-grid">
          <div class="text-column-left">
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Objective</h4>
              <p>
                Machine a high-precision metallic mold insert capable of withstanding cyclic injection molding pressures while achieving tight dimensional repeatability and surface finish. Paired physical milling with computational modeling of cutting mechanics.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Methodology & Technical Execution</h4>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Precision Milling Operations:</strong> Established datum coordinates using a 0.200" edge finder; machined pockets, contours, and parting surfaces with 1/4" and 1/2" flat end mills and a 3/16" ball end mill.</li>
                <li><strong>Holemaking & Threading:</strong> Drilled pilot holes with a #7 drill bit and manually tapped internal mounting threads using a 1/4-20 hand tap.</li>
                <li><strong>Cutting Force Estimation (Virtual Lab 1):</strong> Applied mechanistic machining models to predict tangential and feed forces across varying spindle speeds and depths of cut.</li>
                <li><strong>Surface Roughness Analysis (Virtual Lab 2):</strong> Quantified empirical surface roughness ($R_a$) from profilometry data and benchmarked results against theoretical feed-rate and tool-nose radius equations.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="media-column-right">
            <div class="img-frame">
              <!-- Replace with your machined mold insert photo -->
              <img src="mold-insert.png" alt="Milled Aluminum Injection Mold Insert">
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Tools & Competencies</h4>
              <div class="skills">
                <span class="tag">Manual Milling</span>
                <span class="tag">Injection Molding Tooling</span>
                <span class="tag">End Mills & Tapping</span>
                <span class="tag">Edge Finding</span>
                <span class="tag">Cutting Force Modeling</span>
                <span class="tag">Surface Roughness (Ra)</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROJECT 4: ADDITIONAL TECHNIQUE 1 (ADDITIVE MANUFACTURING) -->
    <section id="additive-mfg" class="section">
      <div class="project-card">
        <h2>🧊 Additive Manufacturing & Anisotropic Build Optimization</h2>
        <h3>Fused Deposition Modeling (FDM), Slicing Parameters & Layer Adhesion</h3>
        
        <div class="card-layout-grid">
          <div class="text-column-left">
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Objective</h4>
              <p>
                Evaluate how print orientation, infill geometry, and thermal extrusion parameters affect mechanical strength, dimensional accuracy, and build time in polymer rapid prototyping for biomedical and mechanical housings.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Methodology & Technical Execution</h4>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Build Orientation & Anisotropy:</strong> Analyzed interlayer shear weakness by printing test coupons across XYZ orientations to align tensile load paths with continuous raster lines.</li>
                <li><strong>Toolpath & Slicing Optimization:</strong> Tuned wall perimeter counts, gyroid vs. rectilinear infill density, and support interface clearance to balance structural rigidity against material waste.</li>
                <li><strong>Thermal & Dimensional Compensation:</strong> Calibrated nozzle temperatures and accounted for polymer shrinkage to ensure press-fit bearings and fasteners seated cleanly post-print.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="media-column-right">
            <div class="img-frame">
              <!-- Replace with your 3D printed prototype or slicer preview -->
              <img src="additive-mfg.png" alt="3D Printed Prototype and Slicer Toolpath">
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Tools & Competencies</h4>
              <div class="skills">
                <span class="tag">FDM 3D Printing</span>
                <span class="tag">Design for Additive (DfAM)</span>
                <span class="tag">Slicing & G-Code</span>
                <span class="tag">Anisotropy Analysis</span>
                <span class="tag">Rapid Prototyping</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PROJECT 5: ADDITIONAL TECHNIQUE 2 (CNC TURNING & LATHE OPERATIONS) -->
    <section id="cnc-turning" class="section">
      <div class="project-card">
        <h2>🔄 Precision Turning & Cylindrical Component Fabrication</h2>
        <h3>Lathe Operations, Concentricity Control & Chip Formation</h3>
        
        <div class="card-layout-grid">
          <div class="text-column-left">
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Objective</h4>
              <p>
                Manufacture axisymmetric stepped shafts and threaded bushings on a precision lathe while maintaining strict concentricity, surface finish, and thermal control during continuous material removal.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="info-block">
              <h4>Methodology & Technical Execution</h4>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Turning & Facing Operations:</strong> Executed roughing and finishing passes, facing cuts, and shoulder steps while monitoring spindle RPM and surface feet per minute (SFM).</li>
                <li><strong>Boring & Center Drilling:</strong> Utilized tailstock center drills and boring bars to establish coaxial internal bores with minimal runout.</li>
                <li><strong>Chip Control & Tool Wear Monitoring:</strong> Adjusted feed rates and coolant application to promote healthy segmented chip formation and prevent built-up edge (BUE) on carbide inserts.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="media-column-right">
            <div class="img-frame">
              <!-- Replace with your turned shaft photo or lathe setup -->
              <img src="lathe-turning.png" alt="Precision Turned Cylindrical Shaft Component">
            </div>
            <div>
              <h4>Tools & Competencies</h4>
              <div class="skills">
                <span class="tag">Lathe Turning</span>
                <span class="tag">Concentricity & Runout</span>
                <span class="tag">Feeds & Speeds (SFM)</span>
                <span class="tag">Boring & Facing</span>
                <span class="tag">Chip Mechanics</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="next-page-container">
      <p>Want to see these skills in research?</p>
      <a href="research.html" class="next-page-btn">Explore My Research <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
    </section>
 
  </div>

